{
 "meta": {
  "title": "One language, how many peoples — the way in, ranked by reach",
  "why": "Every other surface answers \"what is the way in for THIS group\". A mobiliser with a budget asks the inverse: if I resource ONE language, how many peoples does it reach? 35 languages are the way in to all 369 groups with nothing of their own.",
  "generated": "2026-09-27",
  "grain": "one row per (way-in kind, language); a language offered both as a checked bridge and as a computed candidate appears once per kind, because those are different claims",
  "groups_with_nothing": 369,
  "groups_with_no_way_in": 0,
  "a_bridge_is_not_their_own_language": "Every group counted here still has NOTHING in its own language. This table ranks the second-best answer, and the word bridge travels with every row.",
  "three_kinds": "CURATED — checked for those groups. CANDIDATE — computed from published CLDR data about what the country speaks, which is a fact about the country and not about these people. SIGNED — SUN, a constructed signed language that has to be learned. Never summed.",
  "the_map_link_names_the_peoples": "The link uses ?groups=<slug list>, which filters the map by PEOPLE GROUP, so it shows exactly the groups this row counts. Written first with ?codes=, which filters by language, it drew 55 pins for Mandarin's 36-group row and 57 for Akha's 33 — those extra groups also have nothing but belong to a different row. The map gained the per-group filter rather than the column gaining an excuse.",
  "links": "No new link enters the map here. A bridge language's resources are the ones the pin index already carries for that ISO code, already proved by the verification record."
 },
